340094.statement:
Let $S_n(\!\raisebox{-0.3em}{
 \begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.7pt, scale=0.15]
 \tikzstyle{disc} = [circle,thin,draw=black, minimum size=1.7pt, inner sep=0pt ]

 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, black!40, line cap=round] (0,0) grid (3,3);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (0,1) -- (3,1);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (1,0) -- (1,3);
 \foreach \x/\y in {1/1,2/3,3/2} \node[disc, fill=black] at (\x,\y) {};

 \end{tikzpicture}}
)$ denote the class of restricted permutations represented by the displayed pattern, and let $\mathcal{M}_n$ be the set of Fishburn matrices of weight $n$. For a permutation $\pi$, let $\operatorname{LRmax}(\pi)$, $\operatorname{RLmin}(\pi)$, $\operatorname{RLmax}(\pi)$, and $\operatorname{LRmin}(\pi)$ denote its left-to-right maxima, right-to-left minima, right-to-left maxima, and left-to-right minima, respectively. For a Fishburn matrix, the wNE-cells are its nonzero cells with no nonzero cell weakly north-east of them.

**Jelínek's bijection conjecture.** For every $n$, there is a bijection $\alpha$ between $S_n(\!\raisebox{-0.3em}{
 \begin{tikzpicture}[line width=0.7pt, scale=0.15]
 \tikzstyle{disc} = [circle,thin,draw=black, minimum size=1.7pt, inner sep=0pt ]

 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, black!40, line cap=round] (0,0) grid (3,3);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (0,1) -- (3,1);
 \draw[step=1, xshift=14pt, yshift=14pt, thick] (1,0) -- (1,3);
 \foreach \x/\y in {1/1,2/3,3/2} \node[disc, fill=black] at (\x,\y) {};

 \end{tikzpicture}}
)$ and $\mathcal{M}_n$ such that:

- $\operatorname{LRmax}(\pi)$ is the weight of the first row of $\alpha(\pi)$;
- $\operatorname{RLmin}(\pi)$ is the weight of the last column of $\alpha(\pi)$;
- $\operatorname{RLmax}(\pi)$ is the number of wNE-cells of $\alpha(\pi)$;
- $\operatorname{LRmin}(\pi)$ is the number of nonzero cells of $\alpha(\pi)$ on the main diagonal; and
- $\alpha(\pi^{-1})$ is obtained from $\alpha(\pi)$ by transposing along the North-East diagonal.

The conjecture would give a simultaneous correspondence between permutation statistics and matrix statistics, including the inverse-permutation symmetry. The source presents it as Jelínek's Conjecture 4.1 and provides no resolution here.
